SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"Bangladesh had achieved sustained real per-capita growth and poverty reduction, and macroeconomic policies had kept inflation stable, debt-to-GDP low, and external buffers adequate.","locator":"Lines 239-250","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF assessment with clear macro and development judgments, tied to Bangladesh's 2021 consultation and 2022 publication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targets the 2021 consultation but directly frames the macro baseline entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh's authorities under Hasina-era policy; not a claim about one personal decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same IMF assessment warns that risks remained high and that inflation, reserves, and banking vulnerabilities needed close monitoring."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"The authorities reacted quickly and decisively to the pandemic by announcing support packages worth Tk 1.9 trillion, financed partly by curtailing non-priority current spending and suspending low-priority capital projects.","locator":"Lines 240-241","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IMF statement summarizing a concrete fiscal response and its financing logic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate policy baseline for the 2022 year, though describing the pandemic response that carried into 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities during the Hasina government.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also notes that higher inflation and wider fiscal deficits remained a downside risk."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"IMF directors commended fiscal prudence and low debt distress, but urged revenue administration and tax reform, stronger banking regulation and corporate governance, and action to stem non-performing loans in state-owned banks.","locator":"Lines 249-253","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF executive-board assessment with explicit policy priorities and critique.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the policy baseline relevant to 2022 and identifies active weaknesses during the year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and its economic institutions, not a personal action by Hasina alone.","contrary_evidence":["The same assessment acknowledges strong economic growth and social development, so the picture is mixed rather than uniformly negative."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: 2021 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/002/2022/071/article-A001-en.xml","claim":"Social safety-net spending was budgeted at 3.2 percent of GDP in FY22; expanded programs included widow and old-age allowances, a new urban-poor database, and cash transfers totaling Tk 27 billion for about 9 million people, with G2P and mobile-finance tools used to improve transparency and reach.","locator":"Annex V / social safety net section in the search-result excerpt","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"IMF staff report excerpt is highly relevant, though the full page was not directly fetchable in this environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers FY22 and the 2022 policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities under Hasina; the implementation channel was the finance and social protection apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The excerpt also says health and education spending remained low, so the social-protection gains did not erase broader financing gaps."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Reaches Staff-Level Agreement with Bangladesh on the Extended Credit Facility/Extended Fund Facility and the Resilience and Sustainability Facility","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/08/pr22375-bangladesh","claim":"The IMF and Bangladesh authorities reached a 42-month ECF/EFF and RSF agreement aimed at preserving macroeconomic stability, supporting strong inclusive and green growth, and protecting the vulnerable.","locator":"Lines 243-245","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF statement on a major 2022 policy arrangement with explicit goals and country attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers late 2022 and the 2022 shock environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities under the Hasina government.","contrary_evidence":["The agreement itself implies prior macro strain, not a clean success story."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Reaches Staff-Level Agreement with Bangladesh on the Extended Credit Facility/Extended Fund Facility and the Resilience and Sustainability Facility","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/08/pr22375-bangladesh","claim":"Key elements of the program included higher revenue mobilization, spending rationalization, modernizing the monetary policy framework, and increasing exchange-rate flexibility.","locator":"Lines 254-256","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF summary of the program's policy package, directly tied to 2022 decision-making.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 stabilization response.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities; implementation depended on the Hasina government and the central bank.","contrary_evidence":["The need for a Fund program indicates that existing policy settings had not been sufficient on their own."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Helps Bangladesh Build Economic Resilience","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/04/19/world-bank-helps-bangladesh-build-economic-resilience","claim":"The World Bank's $250 million credit was designed to help Bangladesh strengthen its fiscal and financial sector, streamline bank recovery plans, and support payments legislation that would make the financial system more efficient.","locator":"Lines 24-26","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank release with specific financing terms and policy content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 reform implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Government of Bangladesh under Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same release frames the measure as support for resilience after COVID-19, implying ongoing fragility."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Helps Bangladesh Build Economic Resilience","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/04/19/world-bank-helps-bangladesh-build-economic-resilience","claim":"The program supported National Tariff Policy modernization, digital technologies, e-GP procurement, and faster social-protection delivery through G2P cash transfers with gender-disaggregated data.","locator":"Lines 28-30","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank release with concrete policy mechanisms and implementation channels.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 policy design and implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and line ministries that implemented the program.","contrary_evidence":["The press release does not prove full implementation or distributional success on its own."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strong Trade Competitiveness, Financial Sector, and Well-functioning Cities are Critical for Sustained Growth in Bangladesh: World Bank","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/09/29/strong-trade-competitiveness-financial-sector-and-well-functioning-cities-are-critical-for-sustained-growth-in-banglades","claim":"The World Bank said Bangladesh needed a strong reform agenda to sustain growth, with key barriers in trade competitiveness, financial-sector vulnerabilities, and orderly urbanization.","locator":"Lines 24-26","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank assessment of Bangladesh's 2022 growth constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 policy conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level policy environment under the Hasina government.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes Bangladesh had been among the top 10 fastest-growing economies over the prior decade."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strong Trade Competitiveness, Financial Sector, and Well-functioning Cities are Critical for Sustained Growth in Bangladesh: World Bank","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/09/29/strong-trade-competitiveness-financial-sector-and-well-functioning-cities-are-critical-for-sustained-growth-in-banglades","claim":"High tariffs, trade costs, weak bank asset quality, and the need for more finance for women, MSMEs, and FDI were identified as key obstacles to long-term growth and inclusion.","locator":"Lines 29-34","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank text with concrete economic mechanisms and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 structural constraints.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh's policy environment under Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes rapid digital-payment adoption and a large formal economy in Dhaka, so the system was not uniformly closed to market development."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh announces fuel price jump, stokes inflation fears","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-06","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/bangladesh-announces-fuel-prices-jump-stokes-inflation-fears-2865770","claim":"Bangladesh raised fuel prices by about 50 percent, inflation was already above 7 percent, and foreign-exchange reserves had fallen to $39.67 billion from $45.89 billion a year earlier.","locator":"Reuters copy lines 428-452 in the Investing.com republication","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting with specific price, inflation, and reserve figures; republication preserves the original wire content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 shock management.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh government and energy ministry under Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["Officials said the hike was unavoidable because of global market conditions, so the action was partly reactive rather than purely discretionary."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Budget Speech","publisher":"The Financial Express","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/budget-2022-23/budget-speech-1654804655?amp=true","claim":"The FY23 budget speech said the crisis response prioritized job creation, low-interest credit, support for low-income and informal workers through wider social safety nets, and greater spending on health, agriculture, education, and rural development.","locator":"Budget FY23 speech excerpts around paragraphs 28 and 54","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct publication of the budget speech text; the underlying statements are primary, but this is still a media republication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 budget and policy choices.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government via the finance minister's budget speech.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is self-justifying and does not by itself prove effective implementation."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Budget FY23: No rise in social safety net allocation despite rise in inflation","publisher":"Dhaka Tribune","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://www.dhakatribune.com/bangladesh/289298/budget-fy23-no-rise-in-social-safety-net","claim":"The budget kept social safety net allocation at Tk113,576 crore, or 16.75 percent of the budget and 2.55 percent of GDP; the finance minister said the scope was being expanded to reduce poverty and inequality, with 29 percent of households already covered and allocations up almost eight-fold since FY08-09.","locator":"Article body and quoted finance-minister remarks","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting with specific figures and direct quotation from the budget speech.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the FY23 budget announced in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government through the finance minister and budget framework.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes that the allocation fell proportionately versus the previous year once budget size is considered."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh appoints Abdur Rouf Talukder as central bank governor","publisher":"bdnews24.com","publication_date":"2022-06-11","url":"https://bdnews24.com/bangladesh/bangladesh-appoints-abdur-rouf-talukder-as-central-bank-governor","claim":"The government appointed finance secretary Abdur Rouf Talukder as Bangladesh Bank governor; the article says he had held senior finance posts and specialized in public finance, budget reform, macroeconomic management, and IT in public financial management.","locator":"Article body describing the appointment and career background","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting on a specific economic appointment with clear career details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 appointment affecting monetary management.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government; the appointment was made through the finance ministry/government notification.","contrary_evidence":["The appointee was a senior civil servant rather than an independent outside technocrat, so the appointment does not prove maximal autonomy."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sustaining Growth in Bangladesh with Strong Reforms","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-04-04","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2023/04/04/strong-reforms-can-help-bangladesh-sustain-growth","claim":"Inflation rose to 9.5 percent in August 2022, the FY22 balance-of-payments deficit reached $5.3 billion, foreign-exchange reserves came under pressure, a multiple exchange-rate regime was introduced in September 2022, and financial-sector vulnerabilities deepened.","locator":"Lines 34-38","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank assessment with concrete 2022 macro indicators and policy changes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 with direct 2022 indicators and policy changes.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh's macroeconomic policy environment under Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same piece notes Bangladesh still remained at low risk of debt distress, so the picture was stressed but not collapsed."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Structural Reforms are Essential to Sustain Growth & Poverty Reduction in Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-10-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2023/10/05/structural-reforms-are-essential-to-sustain-growth-poverty-reduction-in-bangladesh","claim":"The World Bank said extreme poverty had fallen to 5 percent in 2022, showing strong long-run poverty reduction even as sustaining growth would require policy adjustments to deal with the balance-of-payments deficit, inflation, and financial-sector vulnerabilities.","locator":"Story highlights and opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank feature using 2022 household-survey-based data in a 2023 publication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 household outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome under the Hasina government; not a direct claim about her personal decision-making.","contrary_evidence":["The same story says growth would need policy adjustments, so the poverty improvement did not eliminate macro and distributional weaknesses."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPI 2022: Disappointing global and Bangladesh performances","publisher":"Transparency International Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-02-01","url":"https://www.ti-bangladesh.org/index.php/en/articles/story/6629","claim":"Bangladesh scored 25 on the Corruption Perceptions Index 2022 and ranked 147th out of 180 countries, making it the second-lowest in South Asia.","locator":"Article body summarizing CPI 2022 results","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist anti-corruption watchdog reporting the CPI result with rank and score, directly relevant to governance and capture.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective publication immediately after the 2022 index release.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level corruption environment under the Hasina government, not a direct personal attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The index measures perceptions, not adjudicated corruption cases, so it is a broad governance signal rather than a legal finding."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I screened roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Reuters, Bangladesh Bank, budget materials, local reporting, and anti-corruption sources. I kept the strongest non-duplicative 2022 claims and a small number of retrospective 2023 sources that explicitly reported 2022 household-survey or CPI data. I rejected repeated coverage of the same budget speech, generic country pages that added no ruler-period detail, out-of-period 2024-2026 pieces, and sources that were either inaccessible in full or too redundant with the IMF/World Bank reports already retained. I also avoided overusing self-justifying government language when an independent source said the same thing better.

Remaining gaps
The main gap is fine-grained ruler attribution: the dossier shows the Hasina government’s economic posture, but not always Sheikh Hasina personally choosing each rate move, reserve decision, or appointment. 5B.2 is still the thinnest lens because the record shows a technocratic appointment, but not a clear pattern of empowered independent economic professionals versus loyalists. Household-level distribution data for 2022 is also still thinner than headline GDP and poverty data, so more search would help on median wages, rural-urban gaps, and budget execution versus announcement. I did not score.
